id: design.worker-pool
kind: design
title: Worker pool
aliases: worker pool, consumers, fixed concurrency workers
tags: architecture, concurrency, batch
questions:
  - how would you process a queue with N workers?
  - how many workers should consume a queue?
invariant: A fixed set of consumers pulls from a shared bounded queue, so concurrency is a number you chose and can reason about.
answer_30s: Start N workers, each pulling one item at a time from a bounded queue; the pool size is a deliberate limit on concurrent work and the queue depth absorbs bursts.
answer_2m: |
  Three properties make this the standard shape: the concurrency limit is
  explicit (N, not "as many as arrive"), ownership of each item is exclusive
  (one worker holds one job, so no locks inside the job), and shutdown is
  tractable (stop pulling, finish the current item, exit). Sizing comes from the
  bottleneck: if workers are IO-bound, N can exceed core count; if they are
  CPU-bound, N near core count; if they share a database, N is limited by the
  connection pool, which is usually the real answer. Visibility matters as much
  as sizing: queue depth, in-flight count and per-item latency tell you which
  side of the pool is the constraint. The failure to avoid is dynamic worker
  creation per message, which is unbounded concurrency with extra bookkeeping.
when_to_use:
  - background job processing, batch pipelines, event consumers
avoid_when:
  - work with a hard ordering or single-writer requirement per key (partition instead)
minimal_example: |
  data: shared bounded queue (depth D)
  workers: N consumers, each:
      loop:
          job = queue.recv()          # blocks under backpressure
          handle(job)                 # idempotent, bounded
          acknowledge(job)
      on shutdown: stop pulling, finish current item, exit
failure_modes:
  - unbounded worker creation per message
  - pool size set without checking the shared database's connection limit
  - no queue-depth metric, so the pool's saturation is guessed
follow_ups:
  - how do you scale the pool without multiplying database load?
related:
